  "content": {
    "id": "p34-q15-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "15",
    "prompt": "What three-digit number (in base 10) is 11 times the sum of its digits?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$198$.",
      "worked": "Let the hundreds, tens and units digits be $a$, $b$ and $c$, respectively, where $1\\le a\\le9$ and $0\\le b,c\\le9$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}100a+10b+c&=11(a+b+c)\\\\100a+10b+c&=11a+11b+11c\\\\89a&=b+10c\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $b+10c\\le99$, the hundreds digit must be $a=1$. Hence $10c+b=89$, so $c=8$ and $b=9$. The number is $198$.\n\nCheck: $11(1+9+8)=11\\times18=198$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
